  <iati-identifier>XM-DAC-41123-PROJECT-240280</iati-identifier>
  <reporting-org ref="XM-DAC-41123" type="40">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">United Nations Industrial Development Organization</narrative>
  </reporting-org>
  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Transferring Japanese 3D-printed prosthetics technology to support landmine victims in Africa</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">The project is designed to transform the landscape of prosthetic access and rehabilitation in Africa, with a pioneering focus on Nigeria, a country profoundly affected by the repercussions of conflict, including a high prevalence of amputations due to landmines and unexploded ordnance. By harnessing the potential of 3D printing technology, the initiative seeks to drastically enhance the production, customization, and distribution of prosthetic limbs, thereby addressing critical gaps in the current healthcare system&apos;s ability to support amputees.  Central to the project&apos;s strategy is the development and transfer of 3D printing technology to local healthcare providers and specialists in Nigeria. This approach is set to revolutionize prosthetic services by significantly reducing the time and cost associated with traditional prosthetic manufacturing methods. Through the creation of customized, well-fitting prosthetic limbs, the project aims to ensure that amputees, including children who require frequent adjustments as they grow, can receive timely and effective rehabilitation support.  An integral component of the project involves comprehensive training programs for local healthcare professionals and technicians, equipping them with the skills necessary to operate advanced 3D printing equipment and software. This</narrative>
  </description>
